.TH WS 3
.SH NAME
ws \- WebSocket connections
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a #I /net

.B /net/ws/clone
.BI /net/ws/ n /ctl
.BI /net/ws/ n /data
.BI /net/ws/ n /local
.BI /net/ws/ n /remote
.BI /net/ws/ n /status
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
On p9web,
.B #I
has one protocol,
.BR ws ,
whose connections are the browser's WebSockets.
As with
.IR ip (3),
opening
.B clone
makes a connection,
.IR n ,
and gives its
.BR ctl ;
reading
.B ctl
gives
.IR n .
The control message
.BI connect " host" ! path
opens a WebSocket to
.BI wss:// host / path\f1,
or
.B ws://
for a host on this machine, and the write returns when it is open or has failed.
.B hangup
closes it.
Each write to
.B data
is one binary message, and reads give the messages that come, a read's worth at a time;
after the far end closes, reads return 0 bytes.
.B status
gives the connection's state.
.PP
So
.IR dial (2)
reaches a 9P server over a WebSocket, and
.IR srv (4)
posts it:
.EX
	srv ws!example.org!9p ex
	mount /srv/ex /n/ex
.EE
.PP
The site is a machine that
.IR import (4)
takes trees from:
its
.B /9p
speaks
.IR exportfs (4)'s
handshake, and its trees are
.BR /mnt/web ,
its
.IR webfs (4),
and
.BR /net .
Webfs's requests are made by the server, not the browser, so the browser's
cross-origin rules do not stop them.
It fetches only from the hosts it allows, at first
.B api.anthropic.com
and
.BR opencode.ai ,
and holds no keys.
The site's sign-in comes before
.B /9p
answers, so import needs no authentication of its own:
.EX
	import -A ws!$site!9p /mnt/web
.EE
The page sets
.B $site
to its own host.
.IR Sibyl 's
servers and
.IR searchfs (4)
run in a name space where this has been done.
.SH SOURCE
.B k/src/kernel/netws.ts
.br
.B k/src/web9p.ts
.br
.B k/src/site9p.ts
.br
.B site/worker.js
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ip (3),
.IR import (4),
.IR srv (4),
.IR webfs (4),
.IR searchfs (4)
.SH BUGS
A connection that the site drops takes its mounts with it.
